[00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:35.000]   We are really pleased with the results and confident in a bright future for Ergestone.
[00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:38.000]   While we are sorry to see our industry in such distress,
[00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:43.000]   it is creating more M&A opportunities than we have seen in quite some time.
[00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:46.000]   We are actively evaluating a number of situations
[00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:49.000]   and expect to have more to consider throughout the rest of the year
[00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:54.000]   as prices remain low and distress situations continue to increase.
[00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:58.000]   We believe that our strong balance sheet and track record as a successful consolidator
[00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:04.000]   positions us well to increase the scale of our company and add value to our shareholders.
[00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:08.000]   With that, Operator, we'll now open the floor for questions.
[00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:10.000]   Thank you.
[00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:12.000]   At this time, we'll be conducting a question and answer session.
[00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:16.000]   If you'd like to ask a question, please press stop on your telephone keypad.
[00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:20.000]   A confirmation tone will indicate your line is in the question queue.
[00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:23.000]   You may press start 2 if you'd like to remove your question from the queue.
[00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:27.000]   For participants using speaker equipment, it may be necessary to pick up your handset
[00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:30.000]   before pressing the star key.
[00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:35.000]   Our first question comes from the line of Brad Heffern with RBC Capital Markets.
[00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:37.000]   Please proceed with your question.
[00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:40.000]   Hi, morning, everyone. Hope you're doing well.
[00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:46.000]   I guess on the 2021 color that you gave about being able to keep production flat with the 11 ducks,
[00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:57.000]   is that more just meant to be instructive or is that actually how you're thinking about the 2021 plan as we fit here today?
[00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:01.000]   Yes. A little bit of both, Brad.
[00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:07.000]   You know, it does depend on where commodity prices head towards the end of the year,
[00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:14.000]   whether we want to pick up a rig or not. I think we've been pretty vocal or consistent about our message that, you know,
[00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:22.000]   we need mid-45 to $50 oil to want to go back to spending large capital dollars.
[00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:27.000]   But outside of that, at some point into this year, early next year,
[00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:35.000]   completing those wells will allow us to main production flat year over year.
[00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:39.000]   Okay, good. And then obviously you made a comment on M&A.
[00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:49.000]   Can you go into a little more detail about sort of what opportunities you're seeing and also any thoughts you have about an ability to fund them?
[00:19:49.000 --> 00:20:04.000]   The opportunities are increasing and size is variable from small to quite large as you've seen, you know, distressed and bankrupt companies.
[00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:14.000]   I think all of this will result in further opportunities in terms of larger companies selling assets.
[00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:24.000]   It is difficult with no public equity and, for that matter, debt markets open or open only to a very few.
[00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:33.000]   Maybe we're one of those few, but we've had discussions with financial parties who are interested in participating with us on acquisitions.
[00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:41.000]   And much like we've done in our history, you know, we'll bring in parties at the right time to look at buying assets.
[00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:54.000]   We're going to maintain a very healthy leverage amount and keep ourselves from getting into a distress no matter where we are in the cycle.
[00:20:54.000 --> 00:21:05.000]   So I think we're just going to see more and more opportunities and we'll figure out how we can finance those and make sure we keep our balance sheet clean.
[00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:09.000]   Okay, thanks for the comments.
[00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:15.000]   Thank you. Our next question comes from the line of Neil Dingman with Truist Security. Please proceed with your question.
[00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:21.000]   Good morning all. Robert, first question also on that same line on 21 Expectations.
[00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:28.000]   I was just wondering, you know, why I realized this plan really, I think, you know, at least the one you just initial laid out assumes no new wells.
[00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:41.000]   I'm just wondering what's driving this low cost or another way maybe ask is how you think about maintenance capex, you know, given just so low because, you know, if I recall, I thought Frank already had you all cancel those country club and other memberships.
[00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:46.000]   I just know what else is there.
[00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:52.000]   Yes, there's no country club memberships up here.
[00:21:52.000 --> 00:22:04.000]   Well, I mean, the 11 wells we have are in really good areas and they they will help us keep production relatively flat at $30 million.
[00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:08.000]   Now is that sustainable? Absolutely not.
[00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:11.000]   You know, we still have a relatively high decline rate.
[00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:21.000]   And it does depend on when we brought those wells online. If we wait until the very end of 21, we're going to have a hard time keeping production relatively flat.
[00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:27.000]   If we brought them on today, you know, we might have the same problem because we get all this flush production from these new wells.
[00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:42.000]   So, you know, maintenance to maintain, you know, 13,000 BoE a day kind of level longer term, we're going to have to have a rig running and one rig program.
[00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:52.000]   I don't know what it would exactly cost us today, but generally speaking is 120 million to maybe 140 million, depending on lateral length and working interest and things like that.
[00:22:52.000 --> 00:23:02.000]   So, you know, this is just a very preliminary. If the world stays the same and we're at 40 low $40 environment, we're not spending any drilling capital.
[00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:07.000]   These 11 ducks will help us keep production flat.
[00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:29.000]   Okay. And then you kind of hit on my last one just on how do you think about sort of PDP decline, you know, on a go forward is now that you've been slowing a bit is that that too, you know, going to go down or I guess look at another way is it, you know, you're able to give us kind of how many how many wells would offset that.
[00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:37.000]   I mean, in any way, you can kind of give us a color on how, you know, kind of you're thinking about that in 21 on broader terms.
[00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:58.000]   Yeah, I think we've been consistent in telling everyone that our sort of basic line starting today will call it, you know, is about 30% in the first year and then it gets into the mid to maybe 26 or 27% the next year and then low 20s or high teens after that.
[00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:03.000]   And so, you know, I don't think that's changed any.
[00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:12.000]   And if we didn't spend any capital, then you can kind of forecast what our low down production would look like.
[00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:21.000]   Even if we didn't do those 11 wells, but again with those 11 wells, it'll help keep 21 flat and then going forward in 22 and beyond.
[00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:24.000]   It's all going to depend on price.
[00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:29.000]   Very good. Thank you.
[00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:36.000]   Thank you. Our next question comes from line of Jeff graph with Northland capital markets. Please proceed with your question.
[00:24:36.000 --> 00:25:00.000]   I guess I was wondering, Robert or maybe from Mark, how important do you guys do you think precast flow positive in the context of evaluating, you know, whether to go after this, the Dexton and potentially drilling or I guess some point in time, but would you view that as kind of a, you know, a must if you will, or would you guys have any level of comfort with any, you know, near medium term
[00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:07.000]   outstand, if it helps, you know, if the returns were good and it would help kind of, you know, jumpstart a return to growth.
[00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:15.000]   Sure. Maybe I'll try to answer that one and Robert can add on if he wants to, you know, we keep things pretty simple.
[00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:22.000]   At the end of the day, we feel like our responsibility is to make good individual decisions at the wellhead.
[00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:31.000]   That's why we were quick to put the rig down and stop completing in March and April is because the economics didn't make a whole lot of sense then.
[00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:42.000]   Do the ducks make sense right now? I mean, economically, you definitely could go complete the ducks right now and have pretty nice IRRs for the incremental cost to complete them.
[00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:52.000]   In terms of, are we so focused on quarter to quarter, year to year pre-cash flow that we won't outspend? No, we're not.
[00:25:52.000 --> 00:26:02.000]   And from a practical matter, and we're just going to be pre-cashable positive this year and next year, kind of irrespective of anything else that happens with prices, if all we're doing is completing the ducks.
[00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:15.000]   At some point, we do anticipate picking the rig back up and there's obviously a lag there between the cost you expend to drill in the complete wells versus the cash flow.
[00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:26.000]   Does that mean over the course of the year we're not pre-cashable positive? No, not necessarily, but we're not so fixated on checking that box every quarter.
[00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:38.000]   We want to make smart economic decisions as the wellhead that will flow into the bigger picture. And having some outspend for some period of time, we still think it's supported by the economics.
[00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:47.000]   At the same time, we're not ones just to go keep drilling and completing because we have hedges in place or for other reasons.
[00:26:47.000 --> 00:26:54.000]   We're just trying to be as good as we can of the individual decisions we're making at the wellhead.
[00:26:54.000 --> 00:27:07.000]   Yeah, the only thing I'll add, Jeff, is I think we can run a one rig program with the level of production and cash flow we have today and be cash flow neutral on an annualized basis.
[00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:21.000]   Obviously, there's a jump starting the program. We're going to be cash flow negative for a period of time, but I think longer term over a year time frame. Again, depending on what the price is.
[00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:30.000]   Sure, understood. Appreciate those comments. My follow up, and you guys still have a decent amount of non-off exposure in the Permian.
[00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:44.000]   Are you guys seeing any activity levels there and more on the land side? Is there any traction or conversations on swapping any of that out to increase the operation exposure there?
[00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:55.000]   Still working on the trades, but people have been hunkered down in the bunker for quite some time now since at least March.
[00:27:55.000 --> 00:28:10.000]   So nothing's moving very quickly there. We have not heard anything different from our partners in terms of activity in the near term being kind of the remainder of this year.
[00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:18.000]   So we'll have to see as we get closer to year end what their view might be for activity in 2021.
[00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:23.000]   All right. Thank you.
[00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:29.000]   Thank you. Our next question comes from line of Duncan McIntosh with Johnson Wright. Please receive your question.
[00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:40.000]   Good morning, Robert. Good morning. Check. I had a quick question on the balance sheet. You all are doing a great job in the free cash. I look great paying it down.
[00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:46.000]   I'm wondering if you've been in conversations with the banks as we head into the fall redetermination back in March.
[00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:55.000]   You all were early in getting it done in the spring, but back then it looked like things would be pretty dire again in the fall, and it's not as bad here.
[00:28:55.000 --> 00:29:06.000]   I think with the line of sight you'll have on the free cash kind of how any conversation with them to give you any indication as to what the fall redetermination could look like.
[00:29:06.000 --> 00:29:17.000]   Sure. I mean, probably too early to, you know, certainly anything around numbers, but we do pretty rarely talk to banks and we do that normal course irrespective of the environment.
[00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:26.000]   You know, the one thing I can, for us, we are expecting a decrease in the baron base and, you know, obviously we're not drilling and completing new wells and hedges roll off.
[00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:40.000]   You know, fortunately, we feel like we've got enough ability to pay down debt to outpace that decrease and really, you know, we don't see any scenarios that we think are realistic that would be concerning in that regard.
[00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:47.000]   I will say, like, does the environment feel better now than it did three months ago? Absolutely.
[00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:58.000]   And maybe some of that's the shock of seeing minus 37-dollar oil and, you know, teeing realizations for the months of April and May.
[00:29:58.000 --> 00:30:13.000]   At the end of the day, it's still not a very good price environment for companies in general. And the banks, maybe they feel a little better because we're at $45 or $42 versus somewhere in the teens.
[00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:24.000]   But they still have a lot of issues to deal with and obviously there's been a lot of E&P companies that have gone bankrupt and there's certainly more that will.
[00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:29.000]   The banks still have their hands full and they're still working through all these situations.
[00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:39.000]   Yeah, I think there is a lot of trepidation in the banks part in general and, yeah, I don't frankly see them letting up a whole bunch in the fall.
[00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:43.000]   No, that being said, that's a general statement.
[00:30:43.000 --> 00:30:55.000]   Yeah, we've got one-time leverage and liquidity and generally are viewed as having done the right thing from a financial standpoint. And our assets are better than many.
[00:30:55.000 --> 00:31:03.000]   We probably feel like we've got a pretty supportive bank group that is going to be reasonable and fair.
[00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:09.000]   And historically, that's been the case. But too early to circle a number.
[00:31:09.000 --> 00:31:20.000]   Thanks, Mark. And then, I guess just for my follow-up, Robert, you gave some really good color in order to do some pretty creative things to get LOE down in the second quarter.
[00:31:20.000 --> 00:31:26.000]   I know you talked about how it's going to come back up a little bit here in the back half of the year towards a more historical range.
[00:31:26.000 --> 00:31:37.000]   But any of those initiatives that you took, do you think that you can carry forward to kind of help mitigate that LOE group?
[00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:46.000]   Yeah, I've definitely done. It's just a constant progression of a well's life cycle to adjust artificial lift.
[00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:53.000]   A lot of our wells are on gas lift, so relatively expensive compressors.
[00:31:53.000 --> 00:32:02.000]   Over time, that gets changed out to other lift methods, in some cases, rod pump and other cases, plunger lift, what have you.
[00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:08.000]   We're continuing to work through that, and that will definitely help reduce LOE.
[00:32:08.000 --> 00:32:20.000]   And then some of the things that we were fortunate in getting done in the quarter, working with the utility company to get meters set and get ahead of the schedule there,
[00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:30.000]   or got moved up in the schedule from some good relationships, we ended up getting generators off of our payroll, and those are very, very expensive.
[00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:33.000]   So we didn't have a whole lot, but they still show up as a big expense.
[00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:46.000]   So some of these things we did in the second quarter are sustainable, and we'll continue doing the right things in the way we operate our business and continue to watch costs like a hawk.
[00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:53.000]   All right. Thank you all very much, and congratulations to Strong Quarter, and a strong outlook.
[00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:54.000]   Thank you.
[00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:55.000]   Thanks, Tom.
[00:32:55.000 --> 00:33:01.000]   Thank you. Ladies and gentlemen, as a reminder, if you'd like to join the question queue, please press star 1 on your telephone keypad.
[00:33:01.000 --> 00:33:08.000]   Our next question comes from a line of Noel Parks with Kokor and Palmer. Please proceed with your question.
[00:33:08.000 --> 00:33:11.000]   Hey, good morning.
[00:33:11.000 --> 00:33:13.000]   I know.
[00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:25.000]   I, you know, I was thinking about having the buck inventory that, you know, where it works out that just completing those next year can help you keep production flat.
[00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:31.000]   That's, you know, that's a lot of really great flexibility you have there.
[00:33:31.000 --> 00:33:59.000]   And I mean, was that just sort of a result of the timing of everything, you know, kind of falling off the rails with, with COVID and over Russia, or was that sort of part of the plan as you were just trying to, you know, continue to pivot to emphasis on free cash flow hitting it next year?
[00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:13.000]   I'd love to tell you, Noel, that we have this great plan that no matter what happened in the world, oil going to 20 or sub 20, that we could keep production flat for three years running with various amounts of capital.
[00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:25.000]   If you go back and think about the beginning of the year and the call we had, you know, related to the fourth quarter earnings and full year, we had a one rate program.
[00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:31.000]   We're going to average for the entire year a tiny bit of free cash flow.
[00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:34.000]   And those 11 wells would have been completed.
[00:34:34.000 --> 00:34:38.000]   I don't know if by now I can't remember, but pretty darn close.
[00:34:38.000 --> 00:34:54.000]   So I'd say it's a fallout of what's happened here and just being very conservative with our capital expenditures and cash and making sure that, you know, we come out of this down.
[00:34:54.000 --> 00:35:13.000]   This near term down cycle, we've been in a down cycle since 2014, but at least this recent piece of it that we, you know, we survived nicely through it and come out the other side and continue to find ways to consolidate and grow.
[00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:15.000]   Great. Thanks.
[00:35:15.000 --> 00:35:38.000]   You know, just a housekeeping question, sorry if you touched on this earlier and I missed it. For the free cash flow calculation, if I looked at the numbers right, it looked like this is one of those periods where there is a pretty big delta between, I guess you'd say, cash capex and, you know, sort of capex incurred in the quarter.
[00:35:38.000 --> 00:35:57.000]   And I was just wondering, for the free cash flow calculation going forward, there's just kind of which one of those is more important for us to use as we, you know, just try to model that.
[00:35:57.000 --> 00:36:13.000]   We always use accrued cash from a calculation standpoint because not all the bills necessarily have hit, but we know what generally they are. So, okay, it's in the presentation on the last page or something like that.
[00:36:13.000 --> 00:36:36.000]   Okay. So, oh, and one other thing, and I just don't have a release in front of me, and as far as change in working capital, that's something else you would routinely include, well, include the impact of in a free cash calculation or would you exclude that?
[00:36:36.000 --> 00:36:51.000]   Yeah, so we don't include that, and I recognize there's, you know, multiple different ways to look at that. I mean, the way we've been approaching it is, okay, adjusted EBITAC is the best proxy for, you know, really sort of cash flow for the period.
[00:36:51.000 --> 00:36:58.000]   We back out interest expense, and then we back out capital expenditures for activity during that period.
[00:36:58.000 --> 00:37:10.000]   Now, when you're stopping or starting, there is going to be a bigger delta between calculate and pre-cash flow the way we do versus if you're using a cash-cash-ex number.
[00:37:10.000 --> 00:37:26.000]   You know, the second quarter was very atypical from the standpoint of, you know, here we're reporting $35 million of free cash flow, yeah, our debt balance went up, and the reason for that is because, and of course this is tied to the cash-cash-ex.
[00:37:26.000 --> 00:37:35.000]   It's just the time of the payments. You know, we reduced the negative working capital deficit by about $50 million during the quarter.
[00:37:35.000 --> 00:37:46.000]   Now we're to a point where that working capital deficit is pretty low and probably won't fluctuate a whole bunch one way or the other until we pick activity back up.
[00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:57.000]   At which point you'd expect, you know, as you start completions and drilling again, you start getting bills, but there's some lag between the activity and when you get the bills and when you pay them.
[00:37:57.000 --> 00:38:09.000]   So very typical quarter, yeah, as we mentioned, the press release, you know, we had about $169 million of debt at quarter end, and the month of July we paid down a little more than $14 million.
[00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:25.000]   That's not the run rate, by the way. It's not 14 million a quarter. That was high because of timing, but we do expect, I mean, really every month this year that we're paying down some debt without any big changes in the working capital status.
[00:38:25.000 --> 00:38:31.000]   Great. Thanks a lot for the details. That's not for me.
[00:38:31.000 --> 00:38:38.000]   Thank you. Ladies and gentlemen, that concludes our question and answer session. I'll turn the floor back to Mr. Anderson for any final comments.
[00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:48.000]   We appreciate everybody joining us and, you know, we'll continue to update as we need to and we'll see you next quarter. Thanks.
[00:38:48.000 --> 00:38:54.000]   Thank you. This concludes today's conference. You may disconnect your lines at this time. Thank you for your participation.
[00:39:08.000 --> 00:39:13.000]   Thank you.
